"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was 3.8, and the surgical specimen showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with positive surgical margins, but no extraprostatic extension or seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, which widens uncertainty as nodal sampling was not performed. The biopsy finding of Acinar adenocarcinoma was consistent with the prostatectomy grade.\n\nCountervailing findings include the positive surgical margins and the absence of lymph nodes removed. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy findings were consistent, which is a positive factor. The postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory are unknown, which is a significant factor limiting prognosis assessment. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of local control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and trajectory would be crucial to revise this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 42.17 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.41–56.92 months; its coverage has not been validated."
